= Solution
{parent=c}

Maximal <right ideals> of $R/J$ correspond to <maximal right ideals> of $R$, all of which contain $J$. Their intersection in the quotient is zero. Hence $R/J$ is a <right Artinian ring> with zero <Jacobson radical>, and part (a) makes it semisimple.

Part (b) gives the finite radical filtration
$$
R\supseteq J\supseteq J^2\supseteq\cdots\supseteq J^m=0.
$$
Each factor $J^i/J^{i+1}$ is annihilated by $J$, so it is a right <module> over $R/J$. Every <module> over a semisimple <ring> is semisimple: a free <module> is a direct sum of simple summands of copies of the regular <module>, and a quotient of a semisimple <module> is again semisimple, because the images of its simple summands are either zero or simple and span the quotient. A maximal independent family of these simple images spans: any simple image not contained in the sum intersects it trivially and could be added. Each factor is also <Artinian>, as a subquotient of $R_R$. A semisimple <Artinian module> has only finitely many simple summands, since an infinite direct sum would give a descending chain by successively deleting summands. Consequently every factor has finite <module length>.

Concatenating composition series of these finitely many factors makes $R_R$ a <module> of finite <module length>. Such a <module> satisfies both chain conditions, since every strict change consumes a positive amount of length. Therefore **$R$ is right Noetherian**. This is the <Hopkins-Levitzki theorem>, obtained from the two preceding results rather than invoked in their proofs.

= Solution
{parent=4}

Let $K=k(z)$, where $z$ is an indeterminate, and form the <right Artinian triangular ring that is not left Noetherian>
$$
\boxed{T=\begin{pmatrix}k&K\\0&K\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Multiplication uses the natural $k$–$K$ bimodule structure on the upper-right entry. With the two diagonal <idempotents> $e_1,e_2$, the right regular <module> is $e_1T\oplus e_2T$. The off-diagonal submodule of $e_1T$ is one-dimensional over the bottom-right <field> $K$, hence simple. Its quotient is one-dimensional over the top-left <field> $k$, hence simple. Also $e_2T$ is a simple right <module> over $K$. Thus $T_T$ has a composition series of length three, and in particular $T$ is right <Artinian>.

On the left, multiplication of an off-diagonal matrix gives
$$
\begin{pmatrix}a&m\\0&b\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}0&u\\0&0\end{pmatrix}
=\begin{pmatrix}0&au\\0&0\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Every $k$-subspace $U\subseteq K$ therefore yields a <left ideal> of off-diagonal matrices with entries in $U$. The subspaces $U_m=\operatorname{span}_k\{1,z,\ldots,z^m\}$ are strictly increasing because $z$ is transcendental. Their associated <left ideals> violate the <ascending chain condition>. Hence **$T$ is not left Noetherian**, even though it is right Artinian and right Noetherian.

= 5
{parent=Paper 85}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

In a commutative <ring>, if $x^m=0$, then the principal <ideal> $(x)$ satisfies $(x)^m=0$. Thus a <semiprime ring> has no nonzero <nilpotent elements>. Conversely, if $I^m=0$ for an <ideal> $I$, then every $x\in I$ satisfies $x^m=0$; absence of nonzero <nilpotent elements> forces $I=0$. Hence **commutative semiprimeness is exactly reducedness**.

For a <prime ideal> $P$, let $S=R\setminus P$. Every <ideal> $I$ of $R_P=S^{-1}R$ is the extension of its contraction $I^c$: if $r/s\in I$, multiply by the <unit> $s/1$ to get $r/1\in I$, so $r\in I^c$. If $R$ is <Noetherian>, write $I^c=(r_1,\ldots,r_m)$; their images generate $I$. This proves that $R_P$ is <Noetherian>.

The extended <ideal> $PR_P$ is proper. Indeed $1=p/s$ would imply $u(s-p)=0$ for some $u\notin P$, and then $us=up\in P$, contradicting primeness and $u,s\notin P$. A fraction with numerator outside $P$ is invertible, with inverse $s/r$. A fraction with numerator in $P$ lies in the proper <ideal> $PR_P$ and cannot be invertible. Thus the nonunits are precisely $PR_P$, making it the unique maximal <ideal>. Therefore
$$
\boxed{R_P\text{ is a Noetherian local ring with maximal ideal }PR_P.}
$$
To prove reducedness is detected by prime localizations, let $x$ be a <nilpotent element> of $R$. Its image in every $R_Q$ is zero because these localizations are semiprime, hence reduced. If $x\ne0$, its <annihilator> $\operatorname{Ann}(x)$ is a proper <ideal>; choose a maximal <ideal> $Q$ containing it. The zero criterion for <localization> says $x/1=0$ in $R_Q$ only if $sx=0$ for some $s\notin Q$, which would put $s$ in $\operatorname{Ann}(x)\subseteq Q$, a contradiction. Hence $x=0$, and $R$ is semiprime. This argument does not need Noetherianity.

Primeness is not detected in the same way. Take **$R=k\times k$**. Its two <prime ideals> are $P_1=0\times k$ and $P_2=k\times0$, and its corresponding localizations are both isomorphic to the <field> $k$. To see these are all the <prime ideals>, the orthogonal <idempotents> $e_1,e_2$ have product zero, so a <prime ideal> contains one of them and is then the kernel of the other projection. Both localizations are prime <rings>, but $R$ is not prime: $e_1,e_2\ne0$ and $e_1e_2=0$.

